returning home is becoming an elusive  dream for these refugees  they've been living in tents for nearly  a decade here in the city of the hook in  iraq's kurdish region  we have been displaced for eight years  the situation in sinjar is getting worse  how can we think of going back to sinjar  security and safety are two important  elements for us and because of the lack  of these two elements we prefer to stay  in the camp and enjoy its harsh  conditions our hearts are broken for not  being able to go back  most of the nearly three hundred  thousand people here are from the yazidi  minority  they were forced to flee their homes  about ten years ago nearly half of them  live in these tent camps that barely  protect them from the summer heat and  the winter cold the governments in  baghdad and erbil signed an agreement in  2020 for all irregular forces to  withdraw from sinjar to allow the safe  return of those displaced by war  but that agreement is yet to be  implemented  the terms of the sinjar agreement were  not implemented despite the consent of  both governments it seems there are  pressures on the iraqi government to  block the agreement from foreign powers  that support the militia's  non-withdrawal from sinjar  the iraqi government says it's committed  to the agreement and its forces are in  control of sinjar but people here say  armed militias including the kurdistan  workers party or pkk have never left  we as joint operations command did  everything we had to do in terms of  security we are serious about  implementing this agreement but there  are certain things that the government  has to do from our side we will not  allow any manifestation of armed  activities inside sinjar  sinjar is officially run by a joint  administration based in the hook some of  its members are appointed by the iraqi  government in baghdad while others are  locally elected  but the reported presence of armed  groups is making it complicated and  difficult to facilitate the return of  refugees muhammad
 

